1
00:00:25,269 --> 00:00:32,812
[SPEAKER_03]: I have some Nyanavira material.

2
00:00:33,653 --> 00:00:47,998
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in his note on passa, he says, on account of their structural similarity, and I'll say what those two things are in a moment, these two independent patterns appear one superimposed on the other.

3
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:54,915
[SPEAKER_03]: And when there's failure to distinguish between these patterns, the subject comes to be identified with the eye.

4
00:00:55,336 --> 00:01:01,381
[SPEAKER_03]: Those two patterns being, both in visual experience, forms are seen, the eye is unseen.

5
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:10,668
[SPEAKER_03]: And in quite a different way, he says, objects are seen, the subject is unseen.

6
00:01:11,849 --> 00:01:17,554
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't necessarily have a specific question about that, but I thought that might be a good place to

7
00:01:21,103 --> 00:01:25,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Begin the conversation, maybe about, like, practically, you read that.

8
00:01:26,765 --> 00:01:31,347
[SPEAKER_03]: What does... what is to be done on account of that, you know?

9
00:01:33,188 --> 00:01:44,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, I mean, it's... it's a... it's a correct description, but it is sort of the... it's a description that kind of pertains to the end of it.

10
00:01:46,093 --> 00:01:46,854
[SPEAKER_01]: When you... when...

11
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:55,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that pattern, that's not how you start, to kind of build it up towards seeing the pattern.

12
00:01:55,551 --> 00:02:02,897
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... that can be a potential problem, if somebody just reads that and tries to figure it out.

13
00:02:02,957 --> 00:02:13,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Because subject-object, for example, see, you can be thinking now about, aha, right, object-subject's not seen, but you're doing that on your own terms.

14
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:21,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That already, the experience of doing that, is something that you're subjectively taking, unaware of you as a subject, there and then.

15
00:02:22,425 --> 00:02:26,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you're sort of contemplating what he said, but at the arm's length.

16
00:02:28,189 --> 00:02:34,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... instead of trying to figure it out, until it makes sense in that way, more like try to tailor.

17
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:35,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so do I know?

18
00:02:37,515 --> 00:02:38,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see the subject he's talking about?

19
00:02:39,256 --> 00:02:40,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see that as a phenomenon?

20
00:02:41,457 --> 00:02:43,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see that negative aspect of it?

21
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:54,384
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always in the background, always slips through your fingers if you try to catch, you know, to sort of, to kind of put in front of you this sense of I am, sense of subjectivity.

22
00:02:54,644 --> 00:02:56,485
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always, it's always, it's always slipping you.

23
00:02:56,525 --> 00:02:58,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you try to attend to it directly, it's always elsewhere.

24
00:02:59,766 --> 00:03:01,307
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that it's its property.

25
00:03:01,427 --> 00:03:04,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you can then mystify it, as people would do.

26
00:03:05,253 --> 00:03:08,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you realize, well, actually, it can be still understood, because it does appear as such.

27
00:03:09,614 --> 00:03:15,236
[SPEAKER_00]: It appears as something that's always in the background of what I'm looking at.

28
00:03:16,076 --> 00:03:17,056
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just its nature.

29
00:03:18,036 --> 00:03:20,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Always, it's always going to be at the opposite end of wherever you look.

30
00:03:22,418 --> 00:03:31,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding that, those properties, seeing it real as such, as I said before, that's how you get to actually discern that subject.

31
00:03:32,632 --> 00:03:43,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you know, as he describes, that sort of corresponds to eye organs and sights that you see, but really those comparisons are not not as important.

32
00:03:44,357 --> 00:03:55,822
[SPEAKER_00]: What's important is that whether you approach it through eye, organ and sights, or whether you approach it through like a subject-object divide, you follow it through all the way.

33
00:03:57,013 --> 00:04:03,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just for the sake of comparison and getting the satisfaction of, ah, look, this, I saw what, I saw things that fit, that correspond.

34
00:04:04,998 --> 00:04:06,499
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of not the point.

35
00:04:06,979 --> 00:04:16,925
[SPEAKER_00]: That can sort of encourage you to practice, sure, but the measure of fitting, the measure of successful understanding, is freedom from suffering, freedom from subjectivity, in that sense.

36
00:04:18,646 --> 00:04:25,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you thoroughly understand the saḷāyatana, this basis, the sense organ basis,

37
00:04:26,291 --> 00:04:27,552
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be no room for conceit.

38
00:04:28,653 --> 00:04:33,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And whether you actually recognize that that also corresponds to the subject-object divide or not, it's irrelevant.

39
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:34,959
[SPEAKER_00]: You followed it through.

40
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:51,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why these, like, all these different approaches, as I said, inevitably, you'll be drawing parallels and comparisons and so on, but fundamentally, the practice is on the level of following it through, till understanding is developed on account of it.

41
00:04:52,194 --> 00:04:52,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

42
00:04:53,828 --> 00:05:05,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Till the bases are endured sufficiently enough, discerned sufficiently enough, understood as unownable sufficiently enough, or subjectivity is seen as an objective phenomenon.

43
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:15,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by making it as an object, but by seeing it's real as this subjective phenomenon that's always in the peripheral, and as such it's an objective fact that it is there.

44
00:05:17,608 --> 00:05:19,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Discern that as unownable.

45
00:05:19,311 --> 00:05:22,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Discern that as something that cannot belong to you, cannot be in your control.

46
00:05:24,298 --> 00:05:26,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, your sense of self would diminish.

47
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:41,375
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you often translate passa as pressure, which is also quite practical in the sense... Yeah, yeah.

48
00:05:41,395 --> 00:05:43,336
[SPEAKER_01]: ...of seeing that pressure as a phenomenon.

49
00:05:43,956 --> 00:05:45,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's, that's where you... Are you acting out of it?

50
00:05:45,336 --> 00:05:46,857
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that's where you start looking, because people...

51
00:05:46,997 --> 00:05:50,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, people will read about passa, contact, whatever you translate it.

52
00:05:50,918 --> 00:05:52,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they think on their own terms.

53
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:59,260
[SPEAKER_00]: All right, contact, and then this happens, and it's kind of sort of laying it out, almost like a like a experiment in physics sort of thing.

54
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:01,400
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's it's it's completely not there.

55
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:12,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you make sense of things on that level, it's just there's nothing to do with the dhamma, because dhamma only applies on the level that frees you from suffering.

56
00:06:13,802 --> 00:06:15,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, where is the suffering?

57
00:06:15,042 --> 00:06:17,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, suffering is where things are felt, and that's where the pressure is.

58
00:06:19,202 --> 00:06:26,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ah, being pressured, being touched, being under a certain weight or feeling of aggregates, that's passa.

59
00:06:29,831 --> 00:06:32,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your contemplation then should never forget that or put that second.

60
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:47,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And acting out of that has to entail ignoring the past.

61
00:06:47,345 --> 00:06:52,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might think that the past has gone away because I'm...

62
00:06:53,229 --> 00:06:55,471
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm acting towards craving or ill will.

63
00:06:55,491 --> 00:06:55,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

64
00:06:55,711 --> 00:07:02,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... that's like initially, you know, like the... maybe a coarser form of pressure are gone.

65
00:07:02,877 --> 00:07:10,103
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize that even... even a neutral feeling, for as long as you're not fully free from avijjā, will have a degree of pressure.

66
00:07:12,695 --> 00:07:13,916
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, you just have to define it.

67
00:07:15,497 --> 00:07:17,338
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's that kind of the practical side.

68
00:07:17,458 --> 00:07:19,339
[SPEAKER_00]: The practical development is that, like...

69
00:07:22,601 --> 00:07:23,802
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the contemplation.

70
00:07:24,502 --> 00:07:25,883
[SPEAKER_00]: There is collecting information.

71
00:07:27,524 --> 00:07:40,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... fundamentally, the basis that, eh... that is necessary for any of such contemplation to apply is... is something that needs to be cultivated beforehand.

72
00:07:40,932 --> 00:07:41,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to be kind of...

73
00:07:42,719 --> 00:07:46,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowing the intentions behind your actions, that's always going to reveal a pressure as well.

74
00:07:46,841 --> 00:07:52,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're unsure where the pressure is, just ask yourself, what do you want to do?

75
00:08:10,883 --> 00:08:13,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is your mind leaning towards?

76
00:08:13,405 --> 00:08:14,486
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it thinking about?

77
00:08:14,526 --> 00:08:15,447
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it delighting in?

78
00:08:15,907 --> 00:08:16,788
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll find the pressure.

79
00:08:17,148 --> 00:08:25,015
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll find the pressure as a general sort of weather, so to speak, sending you in a certain direction.

80
00:08:26,556 --> 00:08:30,499
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't need to be like acutely felt intense pressure in your chest or something.

81
00:08:30,539 --> 00:08:30,679
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

82
00:08:30,719 --> 00:08:38,826
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just any form of that being under a mental... under an emotional kind of touch, so to speak.

83
00:08:38,886 --> 00:08:39,847
[SPEAKER_00]: It's contacting you.

84
00:08:41,073 --> 00:08:43,455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exerting its force on you, however subtle.

85
00:08:45,016 --> 00:08:51,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's when you recognize that, right, so feeding this, it's what's unwholesome.

86
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:52,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, of course.

87
00:08:54,082 --> 00:09:05,970
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, the precepts are first taken in that external level to continue until you start learning, but once you recognize that the root of unwholesome is really whether you feed the craving, feed the pressure or not,

88
00:09:07,570 --> 00:09:11,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, like, you don't need specific rules, in a way.

89
00:09:12,713 --> 00:09:25,000
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would certainly not be able to break other rules that are defined, because, again, if you don't act out of that pressure, if you don't act out of the craving, you can't do anything unwholesome.

90
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And the recognition lies there.

91
00:09:26,301 --> 00:09:32,464
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, I said that before, for example, I don't know, certain things might be acceptable to do.

92
00:09:33,144 --> 00:09:33,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, say,

93
00:09:34,806 --> 00:09:36,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you can eat chocolate in the afternoon.

94
00:09:36,687 --> 00:09:37,528
[SPEAKER_00]: That's allowable.

95
00:09:38,809 --> 00:09:40,210
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a kind of simple example.

96
00:09:41,231 --> 00:09:42,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not against the rules.

97
00:09:43,492 --> 00:09:45,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there are all permutations of vinaya.

98
00:09:46,955 --> 00:09:48,336
[SPEAKER_00]: All traditions agree on it.

99
00:09:49,176 --> 00:09:49,677
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fine.

100
00:09:50,637 --> 00:09:55,201
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see that you would be doing it for wrong reasons.

101
00:09:56,542 --> 00:10:03,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrong reasons being anything from boredom, craving for tastes,

102
00:10:05,188 --> 00:10:09,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for sugar, distraction, curiosity, I don't know.

103
00:10:10,053 --> 00:10:12,315
[SPEAKER_00]: But all those reasons are unnecessary reasons.

104
00:10:13,776 --> 00:10:26,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognition here, it's like that, not like, oh, I mustn't have these thoughts or something, but it's more on the level of, you recognize that you have such thoughts, you don't act out of them, you don't try to get rid of them.

105
00:10:28,715 --> 00:10:29,516
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as it takes.

106
00:10:30,256 --> 00:10:41,446
[SPEAKER_00]: If like three weeks later, still, the only reason you would eat chocolate, it's still, you're unclear about it, and it's still rooted in, I just want to try it, I just want to try it, you don't do it.

107
00:10:42,307 --> 00:10:44,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Five years later, you still don't do it.

108
00:10:47,351 --> 00:10:48,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the criteria.

109
00:10:50,193 --> 00:10:52,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that being established in virtue.

110
00:10:52,235 --> 00:10:55,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Being established in right criteria that defines virtue.

111
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:09,816
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if in two days' time, like, those reasons are gone, and you need to take medicine, or you're sick, or something, you eat it, it's fine.

112
00:11:10,356 --> 00:11:12,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that it's fine, because you saw where it comes from.

113
00:11:13,418 --> 00:11:15,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that sense of safety will be recognized.

114
00:11:16,799 --> 00:11:20,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And your own mind, possessed by Māra, cannot attack you.

115
00:11:20,801 --> 00:11:21,482
[SPEAKER_00]: It will try.

116
00:11:22,392 --> 00:11:32,080
[SPEAKER_00]: But it won't be able to enter you, because you have not acted out of that suitable basis, as in suitable formāra, basis that's suitable for mara's attacks.

117
00:11:33,922 --> 00:11:36,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you know internally now what's right or wrong.

118
00:11:39,006 --> 00:11:43,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So I gave the example, but you realize, yeah, it's on the level of the small intentions.

119
00:11:44,311 --> 00:11:50,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, another typical example would be like, I don't know, something... you hear something and you want to look in that direction.

120
00:11:51,473 --> 00:11:53,174
[SPEAKER_00]: And before you do, it's like, why?

121
00:11:56,835 --> 00:12:07,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it might be all just whatever, to prevent issues from arising or something, if it's something practical, but it might also be, oh, well, I'm just curious.

122
00:12:08,018 --> 00:12:09,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, well, if you look

123
00:12:10,788 --> 00:12:18,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of such reasons, curiosity, carelessness, is there a chance that lust would invade your mind, if you see something that's inciting of lust?

124
00:12:19,230 --> 00:12:27,391
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, there's probably a good chance that will happen, because what I'm about to look at is a group of people, men and women.

125
00:12:27,671 --> 00:12:28,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So why would I do that?

126
00:12:28,732 --> 00:12:29,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I won't do it.

127
00:12:29,912 --> 00:12:31,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but I... No, I don't have to look at it.

128
00:12:32,652 --> 00:12:36,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you do have to look at it for whatever reason, you would look at it and you wouldn't think twice.

129
00:12:38,183 --> 00:12:41,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And by I mean any other reason, any other reason that's not unwholesome.

130
00:12:41,844 --> 00:12:49,907
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody starts talking to you from that group, you would obviously look and talk back and... but see, it was not rooted in that wrong thing.

131
00:12:50,467 --> 00:12:51,928
[SPEAKER_00]: All the externally seems the same.

132
00:12:53,448 --> 00:12:56,549
[SPEAKER_00]: There is... externally, there is no difference in that action.

133
00:12:56,929 --> 00:13:03,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you look because you spoke, but first time, you looked and spoke because you were curious or lustful or whatever.

134
00:13:03,892 --> 00:13:07,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Second time is because circumstances just shaped it that way.

135
00:13:08,069 --> 00:13:10,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is a whole world of difference, internally speaking.

136
00:13:11,913 --> 00:13:21,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Another good example is, like, when suttas often talk about it, like, you shouldn't say anything that's... that's rooted in aversion or ill-will.

137
00:13:22,624 --> 00:13:26,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And before you say something that's rooted in aversion or ill-will, you should reflect.

138
00:13:27,829 --> 00:13:28,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I free from aversion?

139
00:13:29,271 --> 00:13:30,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this coming from aversion?

140
00:13:31,626 --> 00:13:33,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not to reflect, oh, is this true or not?

141
00:13:33,848 --> 00:13:36,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this going to benefit the monastery or not?

142
00:13:37,010 --> 00:13:38,211
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not a factor.

143
00:13:38,251 --> 00:13:39,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the external factors.

144
00:13:40,132 --> 00:13:40,412
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

145
00:13:40,953 --> 00:13:43,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I coming out of place of ill will?

146
00:13:44,515 --> 00:13:49,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Will what I'm about to say be factually beneficial for them, beneficial for me, beneficial for both?

147
00:13:51,941 --> 00:13:53,962
[SPEAKER_00]: If those factors are not met, you don't say it.

148
00:13:56,063 --> 00:14:00,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the whole monastery will sort of go to ruin as a result of it.

149
00:14:01,438 --> 00:14:06,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't say it, because your criteria is your wrong intention must not be fed.

150
00:14:08,803 --> 00:14:14,387
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something, you know, like, you would probably, you know, you'll probably go through yourself, and you might see others do.

151
00:14:14,847 --> 00:14:16,628
[SPEAKER_00]: It always feels justifiable.

152
00:14:16,648 --> 00:14:20,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there's always... people will try to apply this criteria to a certain point,

153
00:14:21,871 --> 00:14:23,852
[SPEAKER_00]: They'll reflect, they'll reflect, but then it will come.

154
00:14:24,093 --> 00:14:28,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, but if I don't say anything now, oh, it's gonna have bigger repercussions.

155
00:14:28,416 --> 00:14:28,936
[SPEAKER_00]: I must say it.

156
00:14:29,477 --> 00:14:38,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, basically, what you effectively do then, is like, I must put Buddha's instruction second, and deal with this issue first, and then I'll get back to practicing the Buddha's instruction.

157
00:14:39,504 --> 00:14:41,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize that's why you never practice the Buddha's instruction.

158
00:14:41,966 --> 00:14:43,047
[SPEAKER_00]: You're never taking it on.

159
00:14:43,627 --> 00:14:48,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when it comes too much, you put it aside to deal with it, and then bring it back on.

160
00:14:49,171 --> 00:14:50,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not the practice.

161
00:14:53,156 --> 00:15:01,865
[SPEAKER_02]: I was going to say, like, the smallest of intentions will reveal, like, the biggest things.

162
00:15:01,945 --> 00:15:09,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, like, once you start recognizing those subtle intentions and then not feeding them, the mind will start to rebel.

163
00:15:09,893 --> 00:15:14,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you're going to realize how wild of an animal it actually is, how irrational it's going to become.

164
00:15:16,178 --> 00:15:20,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you see, no, it's fine, look, everything's fine, everything fits, and my practice is good.

165
00:15:20,462 --> 00:15:21,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what do I do next, Bhante?

166
00:15:22,344 --> 00:15:26,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, increase sensory strength, start saying no to yourself a bit more, and see.

167
00:15:26,708 --> 00:15:32,293
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mind remains perfectly equanimous, nothing has changed, congratulations, you've finished your work.

168
00:15:32,333 --> 00:15:33,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Highly unlikely that that would be the case.

169
00:15:34,054 --> 00:15:35,115
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to start to rebel,

170
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:43,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Kick and scream, and even the stuff that was clear and made sense, it's all going to be abandoned, and the mind will just be a raging animal that's going to try to force you to act.

171
00:15:44,242 --> 00:15:47,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Wake you up in the middle of the night, breaking in sweat, just go and act.

172
00:15:47,323 --> 00:15:47,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Just do it.

173
00:15:47,903 --> 00:15:48,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Just do it.

174
00:15:49,044 --> 00:15:50,344
[SPEAKER_00]: It could be completely rational.

175
00:15:51,244 --> 00:15:57,746
[SPEAKER_00]: But the criteria there, by that point, if you have been developing things rightly, you'll know.

176
00:15:57,766 --> 00:15:58,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, how long will...

177
00:15:58,526 --> 00:15:59,727
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't care how long this lasts.

178
00:16:02,887 --> 00:16:06,049
[SPEAKER_00]: My problem is not the fact that the mind is a wild animal.

179
00:16:06,449 --> 00:16:09,331
[SPEAKER_00]: My problem is if I act out of it and keep enabling it.

180
00:16:09,871 --> 00:16:12,292
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's why it's... it's my issue.

181
00:16:12,412 --> 00:16:16,735
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't do my part, it can remain screaming and kicking for the rest of its life.

182
00:16:17,535 --> 00:16:20,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that's still wholesome, because you are not feeling it.

183
00:16:21,537 --> 00:16:29,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously, it can't remain kicking and screaming for the rest of life, because once you... as the suttas of taming the wild elephant repeat so often, once you, eh...

184
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:36,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Pin it down, stop... stop kind of giving in to its... to its whims and pressures.

185
00:16:37,524 --> 00:16:41,146
[SPEAKER_00]: It will kick and scream, urinate, defecate, try to kill you and so on, but it will have to calm down.

186
00:16:43,367 --> 00:16:52,092
[SPEAKER_00]: If the precepts are there developed beforehand, that will be its... its... its leather straps keeping it in place, and it will calm down, and then it will start to listen and take food and obey.

187
00:16:53,993 --> 00:17:00,256
[SPEAKER_00]: How much that elephant will kick and scream individually, that depends how much... how wild was it... it was before, but...

188
00:17:00,927 --> 00:17:09,574
[SPEAKER_00]: As I often say, I reckon today, for most people, it's probably pretty wild, given the just natural degrees of sensuality we all proliferated towards.

189
00:17:11,876 --> 00:17:16,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it does boil down to recognizing the little intentions, the little kind of practical things.

190
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:18,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but everybody says doing this, it's fine.

191
00:17:19,802 --> 00:17:21,404
[SPEAKER_00]: The entire tradition says this is fine.

192
00:17:21,424 --> 00:17:22,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, that's how it's done.

193
00:17:23,105 --> 00:17:25,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally, I just do it like everybody else.

194
00:17:25,367 --> 00:17:27,689
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, that's not the measure of wholesome or unwholesome.

195
00:17:30,302 --> 00:17:40,571
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the basically initial guideline for somebody who really can't tell where wholesome would even begin.

196
00:17:41,291 --> 00:17:53,962
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just stick to it thinking that's it, that's all you need to do, just externally emulate wholesome, accepted behavior norms, and not take on the internal criteria as the actual measure,

197
00:17:55,699 --> 00:17:57,780
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not taking on the practice.

198
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,842
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not even starting it, so to speak.

199
00:18:02,903 --> 00:18:04,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the unpleasant part.

200
00:18:04,043 --> 00:18:16,149
[SPEAKER_00]: See, because you can have your mind revolve around something so silly or irrational for like four or five days, just constantly, because you refuse to look, or you refuse to eat that chocolate, or you refuse to act out.

201
00:18:16,490 --> 00:18:16,810
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

202
00:18:18,010 --> 00:18:22,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, yeah, if that's what the mind does as a result of right course of action,

203
00:18:23,625 --> 00:18:24,406
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the mind does.

204
00:18:24,886 --> 00:18:26,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just focus on the right course of action.

205
00:18:27,988 --> 00:18:32,231
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to be... Oh, I'll beat this mind now twice as hard or something.

206
00:18:33,552 --> 00:18:34,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Strangle it.

207
00:18:34,273 --> 00:18:34,453
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

208
00:18:34,613 --> 00:18:40,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... You know where the gateway is, you know where the door... the threshold is, what you need to protect.

209
00:18:41,298 --> 00:18:44,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is an unwholesome intention, that's what you don't feed, you don't compromise.

210
00:18:44,780 --> 00:18:46,181
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is wholesome, it's fine.

211
00:18:47,382 --> 00:18:50,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind gets elated, mind gets depressed, doesn't matter, that's secondary.

212
00:18:51,165 --> 00:18:52,006
[SPEAKER_02]: You know it as such.

213
00:18:53,178 --> 00:18:54,739
[SPEAKER_02]: As this satipaṭṭhāna sutta says.

214
00:18:58,723 --> 00:19:15,276
[SPEAKER_00]: But generally, it's kind of much easier, you know, as I said, somebody might try to do a bit, but then after a while of that building up of that irrationality and doubt and mind trying to pressure you to act and becoming more kind of seemingly more sensitive, it's kind of easier to just then fall back into, I'll just do this.

215
00:19:15,357 --> 00:19:16,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, see, it wasn't a big deal.

216
00:19:17,398 --> 00:19:19,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally, it was completely not a big deal.

217
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:20,641
[SPEAKER_00]: It was irrelevant.

218
00:19:20,741 --> 00:19:21,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But internally,

219
00:19:23,871 --> 00:19:35,779
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the example with the five animals you're tied to.

220
00:19:37,841 --> 00:19:40,763
[SPEAKER_02]: You try to hold back initially, but then it gets too painful to use.

221
00:19:41,789 --> 00:19:42,710
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I just let them go.

222
00:19:43,010 --> 00:19:43,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

223
00:19:43,710 --> 00:19:45,011
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing in between that you see.

224
00:19:45,031 --> 00:19:45,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

225
00:19:46,172 --> 00:19:47,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's the problem.

226
00:19:47,873 --> 00:19:54,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, if somebody starts to kind of say no on the right level, there is a chance they can discern that middle ground.

227
00:19:55,499 --> 00:20:08,608
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you refuse to even take on the middle level, because it's unpleasant to begin with, and just stick to, I'll let the animals go where others said, where others agreed we could feed, you're never going to take on that internal dimension of practice.

228
00:20:09,209 --> 00:20:10,890
[SPEAKER_00]: That's still about paramāsa in a nutshell.

229
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:19,903
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing the... you're bound by the virtue and duty in that external sense.

230
00:20:20,524 --> 00:20:22,225
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's a factual fetter.

231
00:20:26,063 --> 00:20:45,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And like, and see, you can also see that internally, if whereby, you review the intention behind some actions and you realize, no, this is done for a good purpose, for increasing my practice, for increasing solitude, for decreasing company, for decreasing lust, increasing non-lust.

232
00:20:46,788 --> 00:20:50,889
[SPEAKER_00]: But others might not approve of that.

233
00:20:50,929 --> 00:20:54,010
[SPEAKER_00]: They might find that disagreeable.

234
00:20:56,148 --> 00:20:57,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, like, I won't do it.

235
00:20:59,449 --> 00:21:02,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So then I'll stay in what I factually know it's not as suitable.

236
00:21:04,050 --> 00:21:04,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of fear.

237
00:21:05,450 --> 00:21:10,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of fear of being criticized or blamed by those who obviously don't value the same thing.

238
00:21:12,852 --> 00:21:14,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's that's also a factor.

239
00:21:20,194 --> 00:21:21,814
[SPEAKER_00]: And why would that be a factor?

240
00:21:21,894 --> 00:21:24,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because internally you're afraid to be alone.

241
00:21:27,495 --> 00:21:37,081
[SPEAKER_00]: You're afraid to take it on upon yourself and recognize that it's up to you and nobody else, and nobody can come in between it, which is a good news and bad news, depending on how you look at it.

242
00:21:37,722 --> 00:21:49,629
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are finding shelter and safety, in the sense of camaraderie and group, in that sense, authority, external authority, or it's how you describe it, an impersonal they and them, or we just...

243
00:21:50,725 --> 00:22:05,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's like... that's a big problem, because that will prevent you from recognizing that middle ground, where the actual intentions and that passa and contact is all manifested, endured, and where you are acting from.

244
00:22:07,872 --> 00:22:09,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize the middle ground and then...

245
00:22:10,485 --> 00:22:11,765
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really far-fetched.

246
00:22:12,146 --> 00:22:19,568
[SPEAKER_00]: When you read the suttas, when the Buddha said to some monks or nuns just, whatever you know it's wholesome, cultivate whatever you know as unwholesome, abandon it.

247
00:22:19,988 --> 00:22:21,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And they became arahants as a result of that.

248
00:22:24,810 --> 00:22:28,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I was like, yeah, because they found the roots of wholesome and unwholesome and they actually abandoned it.

249
00:22:30,152 --> 00:22:32,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Means they endured things on the right level for as long as that took.

250
00:22:33,193 --> 00:22:40,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Did not act, discerned, unknowability became apparent, mind tamed, things fell into place.

251
00:22:47,725 --> 00:22:49,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's practice of reviewing your actions.

252
00:22:49,566 --> 00:22:51,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, initially, it will be on that level.

253
00:22:52,767 --> 00:23:01,951
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, say, you are training yourself to become... to delight in solitude, as the suttas would say.

254
00:23:02,451 --> 00:23:10,815
[SPEAKER_00]: To find, actually, to prefer being alone, to not mind living with your own self and your own mind internally.

255
00:23:10,855 --> 00:23:13,136
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when then the same attitude, same efforts,

256
00:23:14,024 --> 00:23:26,047
[SPEAKER_00]: It would apply... and then you would be doing it on the level of recognizing hindrances and acting out of them, because they are obviously fundamentally internally bound.

257
00:23:26,107 --> 00:23:43,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you are withdrawn, when you have not been acting unwholesomely by body, by speech, then, yes, you're going to start seeing just the sensuality on the mental level, delight on the mental level, thoughts of ill-will on that mental level, sloth.

258
00:23:45,234 --> 00:23:51,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And same.

259
00:23:51,637 --> 00:23:56,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize there's a degree of pressure, present, arisen.

260
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:58,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind wants to act, get rid of it, cover it up.

261
00:24:00,062 --> 00:24:01,282
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you don't do it.

262
00:24:01,482 --> 00:24:02,743
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you don't deny it.

263
00:24:04,084 --> 00:24:09,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And train yourself in regard to that newly discovered base, threshold, middle ground.

264
00:24:11,087 --> 00:24:11,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you go beyond it.

265
00:24:14,108 --> 00:24:19,832
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are firmly in the middle ground, they they cannot enter you anymore.

266
00:24:23,014 --> 00:24:30,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, another way of describing going beyond it, being to understand its nature.

267
00:24:30,660 --> 00:24:34,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're acting out of it, that's the antithesis of understanding its nature.

268
00:24:35,405 --> 00:24:36,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

269
00:24:36,445 --> 00:24:39,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so not acting out of it... Or denying it.

270
00:24:39,407 --> 00:24:40,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Or denying it.

271
00:24:40,347 --> 00:24:41,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of acting out again.

272
00:24:42,108 --> 00:24:44,428
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be... that would be the first step.

273
00:24:45,129 --> 00:24:46,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding the nature...

274
00:24:48,130 --> 00:25:01,635
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think you necessarily... most people probably wouldn't... you wouldn't have to make specific effort, because by that time, say, if you are on the level of being able to not act out of hindrances, to see them endure on that mental level, remain composed, that's already.

275
00:25:03,086 --> 00:25:06,267
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not absorbed by the content of it.

276
00:25:06,287 --> 00:25:08,748
[SPEAKER_00]: You're able to see it as a hindrance.

277
00:25:08,768 --> 00:25:09,789
[SPEAKER_00]: You're able to understand it.

278
00:25:20,073 --> 00:25:23,696
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't like the touch, you don't like the pressure that it has over you.

279
00:25:24,056 --> 00:25:24,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to get rid of that.

280
00:25:25,437 --> 00:25:25,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

281
00:25:25,798 --> 00:25:26,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you get involved.

282
00:25:27,219 --> 00:25:29,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Surmounting it is able to... Yeah.

283
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:29,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:33,583
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you are unmoved by the pressure, means you're not...

285
00:25:33,603 --> 00:25:34,364
[SPEAKER_00]: It's no longer a pressure.

286
00:25:34,484 --> 00:25:34,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

287
00:25:35,345 --> 00:25:39,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, how can contact contact the groundless one, as the suttas would say?

288
00:25:40,729 --> 00:25:42,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, contact does not appear.

289
00:25:42,910 --> 00:25:45,993
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there's no more ground for contact that's there to touch you.

